\begin{lemma}\label{lem:boundary-rotation}\label{subsec:affine-rotation}
Boundary rotation satisfies
\begin{gather}\label{eq:web-rotation}
\rho_N^a=(X_1\cdots X_a)\beta_a,\qquad
X_1=\rho_N H_{N-1}^{-1}\cdots H_1^{-1}.
\end{gather}
Also $\rho_NH_i\rho_N^{-1}=H_{i+1}$, with indices modulo $N$.
Thus $\beta_a$ moves the last $a$ input positions to the
first $a$ output positions, which are then wound once.

\par\noindent
\begin{minipage}[c]{.43\linewidth}
On thick edges, use block crossings and determinant dots;
for thickness two,
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[c]{.54\linewidth}
\centering$\displaystyle
\DottedDigon{+}=[2]\hspace{4pt}\DeterminantDot{+},
\qquad\DottedDigon{-}=[2]\hspace{4pt}\DeterminantDot{-}.$
\end{minipage}\par
The thick dots are $X_1X_2$ and its inverse. A vertex meeting
the cut is obtained by rotating its edges away from the cut,
applying the ordinary vertex, and rotating back.
\end{lemma}

statements.proof_tex:
\begin{proof}
Cutting open the rotation gives the braid and winding words.
The product of winding variables passes through a split or
merge, leaving the digon scalar $[2]$ in the last display.
\end{proof}

